# Line endings, decided here rather than by whichever machine last saved a file.
#
# Without this, git stores whatever bytes it is given. Thirty-five files that nobody had
# edited were showing a full-file diff — every line "changed" — because an editor had
# rewritten them from LF to CRLF. That is four thousand lines of noise in a review, and it
# hides the changes that are real.
#
# `text=auto` means: decide per file whether it is text, and if it is, store it with LF in
# the repository while leaving the working copy alone. Nobody has to configure
# core.autocrlf, and a checkout on Windows still gets whatever that platform wants.
* text=auto

# Checked out with the endings the tool that reads them expects, regardless of platform.
# A .sh with CRLF fails with "bad interpreter"; a .bat with LF can misparse.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary: never converted, never diffed as text. Left implicit, `text=auto` would sniff
# each one, and a false positive corrupts the file by "fixing" its newlines.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.svg text
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.mp4 binary
*.webm binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.node binary
